  "claim": 1,
  "title": "Non-parallel shadows = studio lights",
  "verdict": "REFUTED",
  "headline": "In both famous 'non-parallel shadow' frames every shadow line is concurrent at a single vanishing point on the horizon (chi2/dof 1.4 and 1.4) - the signature of one source at infinity - and the down-sun Apollo 11 frame recovers a sun elevation of 15.7\u00b0 \u00b1 1.1\u00b0, matching the JPL Horizons ephemeris value of 15.00\u00b0 to 0.7\u00b0, while any lamp closer than 54 m is excluded at 95% confidence.",
  "impressive": "The sun's elevation over Tranquility Base at 1969-07-21 04:43:31 UTC was recovered to 0.7\u00b0 using nothing but hand-digitized shadow endpoints from AS11-40-5961 (including the shadow of Armstrong's own camera, which marks the antisolar point exactly, independent of terrain), the Hasselblad's reseau-plate calibration, and projective geometry - then confirmed against JPL Horizons. No 1969 set designer was simulating astronomically correct vanishing points to sub-degree accuracy.",
